WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.167 --> 00:00:02.602
-Did you have a good
Mother's Day?

00:00:02.602 --> 00:00:03.636
-Yeah, it was great.

00:00:03.636 --> 00:00:04.970
Yeah, I had a
family brunch yesterday.

00:00:04.970 --> 00:00:06.139
Mom's actually right here.

00:00:06.139 --> 00:00:07.840
-She's at the show today?
-Where is she? Yeah.

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:08.908
-Is that right?
-There she is.

00:00:08.908 --> 00:00:12.044
-Hey, there's your mom.
Hi, mom!

00:00:12.044 --> 00:00:17.851
Hi, mom. Aww. She's beautiful.
Oh, great.

00:00:17.851 --> 00:00:20.753
Is mom from -- Where's mom from?

00:00:20.753 --> 00:00:23.289
-She grew up in Southern Indiana
and then all around the country.

00:00:23.289 --> 00:00:24.991
Her dad was in the Army,
so she's from

00:00:24.991 --> 00:00:27.459
a little bit of everywhere,
but settled in South Bend

00:00:27.459 --> 00:00:28.895
about 40 years ago with my dad,

00:00:28.895 --> 00:00:30.796
and I came on the scene
pretty soon after that.

00:00:30.796 --> 00:00:32.132
-Yeah, exactly.

00:00:32.132 --> 00:00:34.367
You've been busy this past week.

00:00:34.367 --> 00:00:36.436
You got insulted by the
President of the United States.

00:00:36.436 --> 00:00:37.570
-Yeah. That's how you know
you've made it.

00:00:37.570 --> 00:00:39.205
-Yeah. Absolutely.

00:00:39.205 --> 00:00:41.674
He compared you -- He called you
Alfred E. Neuman.

00:00:41.674 --> 00:00:46.612
-Yeah. What do you think?

00:00:46.612 --> 00:00:48.747
-The President of the
United States called you that.

00:00:48.747 --> 00:00:52.251
Wow. What goes through your mind
when that happens.

00:00:52.251 --> 00:00:54.420
-You know, we talk a lot about
elevating the dialogue,

00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:55.989
so I guess the fact
that I inspired him

00:00:55.989 --> 00:00:58.458
to make a literary reference
possibly for the first time...

00:00:58.458 --> 00:01:00.493
[ Cheers and applause ]
♪♪

00:01:05.198 --> 00:01:12.171
-Oh, my goodness.
Oh, my goodness. Not bad.

00:01:12.171 --> 00:01:16.009
Everyone is talking about
your age and how young you are.

00:01:16.009 --> 00:01:17.810
You're 37 years old.
-Yeah.

00:01:17.810 --> 00:01:19.979
-But you were mayor at when?
29?

00:01:19.979 --> 00:01:21.314
-Yeah, I was 29
when I got elected,

00:01:21.314 --> 00:01:22.549
so it's my eighth year now.

00:01:22.549 --> 00:01:25.485
-Wow! And when you --
This is a dumb question,

00:01:25.485 --> 00:01:26.819
but when you're the mayor,
do you give out

00:01:26.819 --> 00:01:27.954
keys to the city
and stuff still?

00:01:27.954 --> 00:01:29.255
-Oh, yeah. Yeah.

00:01:29.255 --> 00:01:30.356
It's one of your most important
ceremonial duties.

00:01:30.356 --> 00:01:31.424
-They still do that?

00:01:31.424 --> 00:01:33.025
-Oh, yeah, totally.
Yeah, it's nice.

00:01:33.025 --> 00:01:35.728
It's kind of gold plated
or at least it looks like it.

00:01:35.728 --> 00:01:38.031
-Is there anyone famous
you've ever given the key to?

00:01:38.031 --> 00:01:40.366
-Yeah. Well, one of the
first ones I did

00:01:40.366 --> 00:01:42.168
was for Jerry Seinfeld,
but I had to trick him

00:01:42.168 --> 00:01:44.237
into accepting it.

00:01:44.237 --> 00:01:45.805
-He didn't want it?
-Yeah, yeah.

00:01:45.805 --> 00:01:47.173
So, I found out
he was coming to South Bend.

00:01:47.173 --> 00:01:49.475
He was performing at our
Morris Civic Auditorium.

00:01:49.475 --> 00:01:51.344
It's this beautiful theater
we have in South Bend.

00:01:51.344 --> 00:01:53.679
And I thought, "This is a major
American cultural figure.

00:01:53.679 --> 00:01:54.980
I'm a new mayor."

00:01:54.980 --> 00:01:56.248
I'm like, "I'd better give him
a key to the city."

00:01:56.248 --> 00:01:57.249
So we had it made up.

00:01:57.249 --> 00:01:58.551
I had a little plaque.

00:01:58.551 --> 00:02:00.419
It said, "Thanks for all
the laughs, Jerry," you know.

00:02:00.419 --> 00:02:01.954
And we said to his team,
"You know, it's cool if he

00:02:01.954 --> 00:02:03.489
wants to do a low-key
backstage thing.

00:02:03.489 --> 00:02:06.826
That's fine.
However Jerry wants to do it."

00:02:06.826 --> 00:02:07.927
For me to give him the key.

00:02:07.927 --> 00:02:09.428
And then the day
the event rolls around,

00:02:09.428 --> 00:02:10.730
and we haven't heard back.

00:02:10.730 --> 00:02:13.199
And, finally, they say, "Oh, no.
He doesn't want to do it.

00:02:13.199 --> 00:02:14.633
You know, it's an election year.

00:02:14.633 --> 00:02:16.502
He doesn't do
anything political."

00:02:16.502 --> 00:02:18.238
-Jerry Seinfeld
said no to the key to the city?

00:02:18.238 --> 00:02:19.639
-Yeah. So, then I went back
to the office

00:02:19.639 --> 00:02:20.740
and I checked with
our secretary,

00:02:20.740 --> 00:02:21.941
who had been there for
like 15 years.

00:02:21.941 --> 00:02:23.342
I was like, "Is there
any record of someone

00:02:23.342 --> 00:02:26.478
ever refusing a key
to the city?"

00:02:26.478 --> 00:02:28.514
-This is such an episode of
"Seinfeld."

00:02:28.514 --> 00:02:29.548
[ As Seinfeld ] He's like,

00:02:29.548 --> 00:02:31.049
"I don't want
the key to the city.

00:02:31.049 --> 00:02:33.753
I don't want to able to get in.
I want to be locked out.

00:02:33.753 --> 00:02:36.655
I want to be locked out
of the city.

00:02:36.655 --> 00:02:40.793
I don't want to be in.
You keep the key."

00:02:40.793 --> 00:02:44.029
-Yeah, but now it's like a
crisis of civic prestige, right?

00:02:44.029 --> 00:02:47.033
Because I don't want to be
the first mayor ever

00:02:47.033 --> 00:02:48.735
to have the key
to the city refused.

00:02:48.735 --> 00:02:50.236
And I'll be dammed if
Jerry Seinfeld

00:02:50.236 --> 00:02:51.471
is going to do that
to me, right?

00:02:51.471 --> 00:02:52.604
So I'm thinking, "All right,
what do we do?

00:02:52.604 --> 00:02:53.973
What do we do?"

00:02:53.973 --> 00:02:57.376
And I got a ticket to the show.
I had the key with me.

00:02:57.376 --> 00:02:59.846
Got there, got a nice seat
close to the front.

00:02:59.846 --> 00:03:01.780
He gave this great stand-up.
His stand-up is fantastic.

00:03:01.780 --> 00:03:03.382
-Oh, he's one of the best.

00:03:03.382 --> 00:03:05.718
-And at the end, there's a --
You know, people loved it,

00:03:05.718 --> 00:03:07.686
so he comes back out, but
there's no encore.

00:03:07.686 --> 00:03:08.921
He just started
taking questions.

00:03:08.921 --> 00:03:10.423
The first question is like --
I don't know --

00:03:10.423 --> 00:03:12.057
You know, "What was Kramer
like off the set?" or whatever.

00:03:12.057 --> 00:03:13.326
And then I thought,
"Now is my chance."

00:03:13.326 --> 00:03:15.929
So I wave my hand real high,
and he calls on me.

00:03:15.929 --> 00:03:17.062
And my question, of course, was,

00:03:17.062 --> 00:03:18.730
"Will you accept
the key to the city?"

00:03:18.730 --> 00:03:21.433
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Yes!

00:03:21.433 --> 00:03:25.204
This guy gets it done.
He can't say no, right?

00:03:25.204 --> 00:03:27.540
-No. He's like, "What's that?
The key to the city?"

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:30.142
And then, I think by the time
he said that, I was up there.

00:03:30.142 --> 00:03:32.345
They couldn't stop me,
'cause it was a city facility.

00:03:32.345 --> 00:03:34.847
Gave him the key, gave a wave,
got out of there.

00:03:34.847 --> 00:03:36.015
Nobody refuses a key
to the city.

00:03:36.015 --> 00:03:41.987
-No, exactly right.
Don't mess with this guy.

00:03:41.987 --> 00:03:45.191
-You're very proud about
your time in the military.

00:03:45.191 --> 00:03:46.758
You spent some time
in Afghanistan.

00:03:46.758 --> 00:03:48.961
Is this -- What's --
-Oh, yeah.

00:03:48.961 --> 00:03:51.898
-This is -- What year is this?
-That was 2014.

00:03:51.898 --> 00:03:53.399
-And do you remember this scene?

00:03:53.399 --> 00:03:55.301
-Yeah, that was probably
the best day of my deployment.

00:03:55.301 --> 00:03:57.736
So, one of the
side projects we did --

00:03:57.736 --> 00:03:59.839
I was stationed in Kabul,

00:03:59.839 --> 00:04:02.241
but I worked with
the chaplain's office.

00:04:02.241 --> 00:04:04.009
People kept sending
humanitarian goods,

00:04:04.009 --> 00:04:07.880
and so they needed people who
were qualified on a rifle,

00:04:07.880 --> 00:04:10.015
'cause you had to have
that qualification

00:04:10.015 --> 00:04:12.451
for some of us to take a
vehicle outside the wire

00:04:12.451 --> 00:04:15.021
in order to get these goods
to this orphanage.

00:04:15.021 --> 00:04:16.289
We had --
I can't remember how much.

00:04:16.289 --> 00:04:19.158
It was like 600 or 800 pounds of
stuff people sent --

00:04:19.158 --> 00:04:21.327
you know,
school supplies and clothes.

00:04:21.327 --> 00:04:22.628
And, so, we went into the city

00:04:22.628 --> 00:04:25.298
and we wound up
meeting all these kids.

00:04:25.298 --> 00:04:28.100
There was a Boy Scout program
going on at this orphanage.

00:04:28.100 --> 00:04:29.434
It was just a reminder that,
you know,

00:04:29.434 --> 00:04:32.004
even in the middle of a war,
you know, most people's

00:04:32.004 --> 00:04:34.907
full-time job during a war
is not to be in a war.

00:04:34.907 --> 00:04:36.208
They're going about their lives.

00:04:36.208 --> 00:04:38.845
And it was a reminder of just
kind of the humanity

00:04:38.845 --> 00:04:40.879
that is there in
this hauntingly beautiful city

00:04:40.879 --> 00:04:43.515
even as this awful situation
was going on around it.

00:04:43.515 --> 00:04:46.585
-Yeah. I mean,
what do you do as a kid?

00:04:46.585 --> 00:04:49.221
-You know, the thing
is -- they're just kids, right?

00:04:49.221 --> 00:04:50.989
They behave like kids anywhere.

00:04:50.989 --> 00:04:53.058
They're running around.
You know, they've got candy.

00:04:53.058 --> 00:04:54.192
They're joking.

00:04:54.192 --> 00:04:55.694
And you just think about,

00:04:55.694 --> 00:04:58.831
"What's the future for
a child in a country like this?"

00:04:58.831 --> 00:05:01.000
But there was a lot of really
good work going on

00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:03.269
with some of the organizations,
like the group called PARSA

00:05:03.269 --> 00:05:05.705
that ran this orphanage.

00:05:05.705 --> 00:05:07.607
And I wonder what happened to
some of those kids

00:05:07.607 --> 00:05:09.242
that are in those pictures.

00:05:09.242 --> 00:05:12.778
-Oh, that's nice that you did
that and gave us your time.

00:05:12.778 --> 00:05:15.514
Now that you're on the road
and you're talking to everybody

00:05:15.514 --> 00:05:17.983
and you're campaigning,
what is the big issue

00:05:17.983 --> 00:05:19.252
that is being brought up?

00:05:19.252 --> 00:05:21.687
-Yeah. Well, we're talking a lot
about democratic reform,

00:05:21.687 --> 00:05:23.623
just because I think all
the issues we care about,

00:05:23.623 --> 00:05:26.125
of which I think climate is
probably the most pressing --

00:05:26.125 --> 00:05:27.626
But it's gonna be hard to deal
with any issue

00:05:27.626 --> 00:05:30.429
as long as Congress is set up
with districts where, you know,

00:05:30.429 --> 00:05:31.931
politicians are
choosing their voters,

00:05:31.931 --> 00:05:33.265
instead of the other way around.

00:05:33.265 --> 00:05:35.000
For my dime,
we ought to elect our president

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:37.270
just by a national popular vote
and just count up the votes

00:05:37.270 --> 00:05:39.371
and give it to the person
who gets the most votes.

00:05:39.371 --> 00:05:43.308
So...
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:43.308 --> 00:05:45.344
So, there's a lot of structural
reform we got to go,

00:05:45.344 --> 00:05:46.545
but also climate.

00:05:46.545 --> 00:05:48.047
Climate's a
life-and-death issue,

00:05:48.047 --> 00:05:49.348
you know, especially for
my generation

00:05:49.348 --> 00:05:50.749
and those coming after.

00:05:50.749 --> 00:05:53.352
For the rest of our lives,
you know, our chances in life

00:05:53.352 --> 00:05:56.322
will be partly decided
by whether or not our economy

00:05:56.322 --> 00:05:58.691
can survive all the climate
disruption going on.

00:05:58.691 --> 00:06:01.460
As a mayor, we've already had to
deal with historic flooding.

00:06:01.460 --> 00:06:03.596
You know, we had a
once-in-a-1,000-year flood,

00:06:03.596 --> 00:06:06.432
followed by a once-in-a-500-year
flood, they told us,

00:06:06.432 --> 00:06:07.767
two years apart,
so it's a huge issue.

00:06:07.767 --> 00:06:09.135
-What can we do?

00:06:09.135 --> 00:06:11.537
-Well, there's a lot of things
we could do right away.

00:06:11.537 --> 00:06:14.006
Major federal investment
in renewables,

00:06:14.006 --> 00:06:16.074
carbon storage, energy storage.

00:06:16.074 --> 00:06:18.944
There's also a plan called
a carbon tax and dividend.

00:06:18.944 --> 00:06:21.447
Basically, you set a price on
things that put carbon

00:06:21.447 --> 00:06:23.850
into the atmosphere,
but then you can rebate that

00:06:23.850 --> 00:06:26.285
back out to the American people,
so most of us

00:06:26.285 --> 00:06:28.654
would actually be economically
better off if we did it.

00:06:28.654 --> 00:06:31.824
Meanwhile, it would help change
the economic incentives

00:06:31.824 --> 00:06:35.294
so that you'd see less activity
that hurts the environment,

00:06:35.294 --> 00:06:38.230
because the true cost is not
reflected in the price of,

00:06:38.230 --> 00:06:40.466
for example,
energy that comes from coal.

00:06:40.466 --> 00:06:43.236
If you're facing
the true price of it,

00:06:43.236 --> 00:06:44.970
you'd have to set that price
a lot higher.

00:06:44.970 --> 00:06:46.205
-Is there it money?

00:06:46.205 --> 00:06:47.206
Is that what you're
really fighting --

00:06:47.206 --> 00:06:48.574
-That's part of it.

00:06:48.574 --> 00:06:50.642
You know, there's, obviously,
a fossil-fuel industry,

00:06:50.642 --> 00:06:53.545
that is really committed to
keeping things the way they are,

00:06:53.545 --> 00:06:55.915
which is why our campaign signed
a pledge that we're not going to

00:06:55.915 --> 00:06:58.250
accept any funding
from the fossil-fuel industry.

00:06:58.250 --> 00:07:03.389
-Really?
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:03.389 --> 00:07:07.159
-Final question. Very important.

00:07:07.159 --> 00:07:09.028
Who do you think will be
on the throne

00:07:09.028 --> 00:07:10.762
at the end of "Game of Thrones."

00:07:10.762 --> 00:07:12.431
-All right, so, we've been
campaigning pretty hard,

00:07:12.431 --> 00:07:14.166
so no spoilers.

00:07:14.166 --> 00:07:16.936
I'm an episode behind.
-Me too. Oh, good.

00:07:16.936 --> 00:07:18.904
-Okay. All right. Good.
We're in the same boat.

00:07:18.904 --> 00:07:21.340
-This is fantastic.
We could talk all night.

00:07:21.340 --> 00:07:23.075
-Up until this year,
this season,

00:07:23.075 --> 00:07:24.777
I was pretty much on
Team Daenerys.

00:07:24.777 --> 00:07:26.913
I felt like she was really
growing in her leadership style.

00:07:26.913 --> 00:07:28.246
-Of course.

00:07:28.246 --> 00:07:31.283
-I'm a little concerned now,
based on what I've seen

00:07:31.283 --> 00:07:32.418
in the last few episodes,

00:07:32.418 --> 00:07:33.819
that her kind of
management approach

00:07:33.819 --> 00:07:36.755
has kind of got some issues.

00:07:36.755 --> 00:07:39.525
-Her management approach.
-I'm kind of back to square one.

00:07:39.525 --> 00:07:40.793
I don't know.

00:07:40.793 --> 00:07:42.895
I'm rooting for Jon Snow --
maybe not the brightest,

00:07:42.895 --> 00:07:47.800
but definitely,
you know, got a good heart.

00:07:47.800 --> 00:07:49.101
-Wow.
-Tough to say.

00:07:49.101 --> 00:07:51.437
-What a great answer to a
"Game of Thrones" question.

00:07:51.437 --> 00:07:53.639
You put a lot
of thought into it.

00:07:53.639 --> 00:07:57.410
I appreciate that. Thank you.
Mayor Pete Buttigieg, everybody.

